{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Your name shall not be called, etc., but Israel.</b> One needs to know what is different about Jacob from Abraham, for one who calls Abraham \"Abram\" transgresses a positive commandment (Berakhot 13a), but one who calls Israel \"Jacob\" does not transgress. And even though our Sages of blessed memory (ibid.) said that Jacob reverted to his former name, why do we not learn from the fact that He restored him to \"Jacob\" even though He said of him ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא״ — just as with Abraham He also restored it?" "Or perhaps, inasmuch as HaShem changed His favorable pronouncement regarding Jacob from what was said regarding Abraham, in that He said ״שִׁמְךָ יַעֲקֹב\" — for there was no need to say thus, for what did He innovate by saying ״שִׁמְךָ יַעֲקֹב\"? Rather, it is certain that He intended to say: a fixed name it is to you. But one needs to know the reason of the Holy One, blessed be He, why regarding Abraham He was particular about \"Abram,\" but regarding Jacob He was not particular. And it would appear that there is a sound reason, and it is based on what we have already established, that the names of human beings are the names of their souls, and HaShem places names on the earth, as the Sages of blessed memory expounded (Berakhot 7b), etc. Now, Jacob is the name of the soul that he possessed, and therefore even though a divine spirit called Israel was added to him, for that reason the first one was not lost — for the first name still exists along with the second, and he can be called Jacob, and it is not fitting that his first name be uprooted entirely. Not so with Abraham, for even after the change of his name for exaltation, the first name was not uprooted, for \"Abram\" is included within \"Abraham.\" Therefore the El commanded to call him Abraham continuously, and there is no uprooting of the first name, for \"Abram\" is included within \"Abraham.\" Or perhaps this is what the verse alluded to in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 1:26): ״אַבְרָם הוּא אַבְרָהָם.\" And according to this, the verse's saying <b>Your name shall not be called anymore</b>, etc., means: not alone, but also Israel. Also, scrutinize His saying ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא שִׁמְךָ עוֹד\" and not ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא עוֹד שִׁמְךָ\" as He said regarding Abraham (Genesis 17:5) — and understand:"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:15.292665638Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}